# Error variable naming

Always name the error variable `err`.

Do **not** use descriptive names like `someErr`, `claimErr`, or `dbErr` for a single error in scope.

Exception: when several errors must be kept alive at once (collecting or joining multiple failures), use distinct names so values are not overwritten.

```go
// BAD — descriptive name for a single in-scope error
claimErr := repo.Claim(ctx)
if claimErr != nil {
	return claimErr
}

// GOOD
err := repo.Claim(ctx)
if err != nil {
	return err
}

// GOOD — multiple errors must stay alive concurrently
var errs []error
if err := stepA(ctx); err != nil {
	errs = append(errs, err)
}
if err := stepB(ctx); err != nil {
	errs = append(errs, err)
}
return errors.Join(errs...)
```

Guidance only. No golangci analyzer required by this rule.
